{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Operation evidence binding one explicitly selected Product Definition, the complete companion population of one exact upstream release-matched cohort, and owner-selected consumer bindings. The caller selects every native route and derived context in scope. Selection, plan and result are neither persistent dependency declarations nor registries nor locks; an empty derived-context population means the owner selected none.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 373,
          "quote": "A Complete Consumer Update Plan binds one explicitly selected Product\nDefinition and the complete companion population of one exact upstream\nrelease-matched cohort to the consumer bindings selected by the\ninvocation's owner.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line": 361
        }
      ],
      "key": "complete_consumer_update_plan",
      "label": "Complete Consumer Update Plan"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The selected Product Definition owns composition; the upstream cohort and each Product's release record own release and member identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 366,
          "quote": "The Product Definition continues to own composition;\nthe upstream cohort and each Product's release record own release and\nmember identity.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line": 364
        }
      ],
      "key": "consumer_update_identity_owners",
      "label": "Composition, release and member identity owners"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The separate cohort-update operation presents a whole plan without consumer effects, then may apply an explicitly accepted, re-derived plan. Its outcome predicates distinguish external effect, admission or construction availability, and conditions evaluated by commands with no intended external effect.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 376,
          "quote": "The separate `cohort-update` operation presents the whole plan without\nconsumer effects.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line": 375
        },
        {
          "end_line": 420,
          "quote": "each\nfor its own predicate; the cause of an outcome selects its value, and the\nmapping adds no core value",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line": 418
        }
      ],
      "key": "cohort_update_operation",
      "label": "Complete update operation and outcome predicates"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "For intended external effects, completed refines satisfied, not_effected refines falsified, and completion_unknown refines indeterminate. Whether the effect's end is established is a separate coordinate: a caught failure remains not_effected even when restoration is unverified. Unused staged immutable installs are recorded as the part that occurred.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 445,
          "quote": "Abrupt process or\n  host loss, where no caught failure establishes that the update fell\n  short, is `completion_unknown` (`indeterminate`), never `completed` or\n  `not_effected`.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line": 442
        },
        {
          "end_line": 438,
          "quote": "Whether its restored preimages are verified\n  decides only whether the effect's end is established, never the value:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line": 436
        }
      ],
      "key": "manager_effect_results",
      "label": "Manager effect result refinements"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A term is a lexical label in an occurrence, not a context-free concept identity. Every material occurrence has a semantic address: term, bounded-context identity, owning authority, selected basis and governed scope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 496,
          "quote": "A term is a lexical label used in an occurrence, not a context-free concept\nidentity.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line": 495
        },
        {
          "end_line": 512,
          "quote": "(term, bounded-context identity, owning authority, selected basis, governed scope)",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line": 512
        }
      ],
      "key": "term_occurrence",
      "label": "Term occurrence and semantic address"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An explicitly identified semantic scope whose governed vocabulary, concepts and relations are jointly interpretable under named authority. PRODUCT_DEFINITION declares ownership of urn:stdo:bounded-context:semantic-resolution under the selected complete STDO basis; glossary indexing does not participate in that declaration.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 493,
          "quote": "This member owns this law in the semantic-resolution bounded context,\n`urn:stdo:bounded-context:semantic-resolution`, under the selected complete\nSTDO basis.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line": 490
        },
        {
          "end_line": 498,
          "quote": "A bounded context is an explicitly identified semantic scope in\nwhich a governed vocabulary, concept set and relation set are jointly\ninterpretable under named authority.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line": 496
        }
      ],
      "key": "bounded_context",
      "label": "Bounded context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An exact resolvable reference locating meaning owned by an authority under a selected basis; it creates neither meaning nor authority. GLOSSARY is a non-deciding locator index whose records point to exact clauses declaring contexts and owning concepts, without defining either or creating a shared default.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 501,
          "quote": "A concept reference is an exact,\nresolvable reference to a meaning owned by an authority under a selected\nbasis; the reference locates that meaning and mints neither the meaning nor\nits authority",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line": 498
        },
        {
          "end_line": 558,
          "quote": "The glossary defines neither and cannot\nrepair a missing declaration.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line": 555
        }
      ],
      "key": "concept_reference",
      "label": "Concept reference and glossary locator"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Four explicit owner-authorised relation kinds: Semantic Import adopts unchanged meaning for a declared target scope; Semantic Translation declares directional meaning change and loss, including specialisation; Semantic Equivalence requires governing authorities to establish equality while each concept retains its owner and context and neither adopts the other's meaning; Semantic Disambiguation selects one meaning for one scope without equating competitors. Translation, equivalence and disambiguation are not imports. A specialisation preserves declared source meaning while narrowing admissible instances or adding target-context constraints.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 593,
          "quote": "| Semantic Disambiguation | not an import: it selects one meaning for one scope and does not make competing concepts equal. Where the selected concept is owned in another bounded context, it enters that scope only by the import, translation or equivalence its carrier declares (§6) |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line": 593
        },
        {
          "end_line": 606,
          "quote": "A specialisation is a directional translation whose target preserves the\ndeclared source meaning while narrowing its admissible instances or adding\ntarget-context constraints.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line": 604
        }
      ],
      "key": "cross_context_semantic_relation",
      "label": "Cross-context semantic relation kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A local axiom, override or disambiguation binds a source-owned decision through a URI, existing lawful authority, applicable overridden clause or resolved ambiguity, and exact scope. A local disambiguation additionally binds an exact lexical term, context, complete material candidate references, a selected member of those candidates, and every semantic basis required for the candidates and resolution. Overlay metadata locates the resolution and makes it checkable; the referenced URI owns it.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 621,
          "quote": "- the URI carrying the local decision;\n- the existing Product, requirement, accepted-design or other lawful\n  authority that owns it;\n- the clause or ambiguity it overrides or resolves, where applicable; and\n- the exact scope to which it applies.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F6.%20Local%20constitutional%20binding",
          "start_line": 617
        }
      ],
      "key": "local_constitutional_binding",
      "label": "Local constitutional binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A Product-owned accepted declaration of the shared frame set through which finite actors collectively engage one governed Product scope or outcome. It binds exact reference-frame law and optional profile basis; admitting authorities and scopes; frames and evaluation inventory; actor-binding rules and capability envelopes; semantic, evaluation, operation and decision grants; result, conjunction, translation and coverage relations; lifecycle activation triggers and invalidation law. The non-empty reference_frame_bases set locates these declarations through URI, every admitting lawful authority and exact governed scope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 652,
          "quote": "A Project Reference-Frame Basis is the Product-owned accepted declaration\nof the shared frame set through which finite actors collectively engage one\ngoverned Product scope or outcome",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F7.%20Collective%20reference-frame%20basis",
          "start_line": 649
        },
        {
          "end_line": 673,
          "quote": "`reference_frame_bases` is the non-empty locator set for the accepted\nframe bases through which finite actors collectively engage the governed\nProduct and its work.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F7.%20Collective%20reference-frame%20basis",
          "start_line": 671
        }
      ],
      "key": "project_reference_frame_basis",
      "label": "Project Reference-Frame Basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An execution-scoped binding of one agent to an applicable frame declaration or active configuration, exact evaluation, subject, basis, evidence boundary and capability envelope. The agent receives the REFERENCE_FRAME §11 activation packet and performs the ACTIVATION §1 step. An authorised ticket or work instruction carries the packet, while the Product Definition records the durable shared basis.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 702,
          "quote": "An Agent Frame\nActivation is the execution-scoped binding of one agent to an applicable\nframe declaration or active configuration, an exact evaluation, subject,\nbasis, evidence boundary and capability envelope;",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F7.%20Collective%20reference-frame%20basis",
          "start_line": 695
        }
      ],
      "key": "agent_frame_activation",
      "label": "Agent Frame Activation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "The governed outcome crosses a chain of Products."
      ],
      "definition": "The collective basis records distinct mutable source projects, Product Definitions, candidate checkpoints, release cuts, released Products, installs, artifacts and dependent Products. Each development product is a role binding over its exact underlying install identity. Directed composition edges name source and target roles, governing authority and contracts, lifecycle, evidence boundary, refusal and invalidation conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 720,
          "quote": "The Project Reference-Frame Basis also records the Product-role map when\nthe governed outcome crosses a chain of Products.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F7.%20Collective%20reference-frame%20basis",
          "start_line": 712
        }
      ],
      "key": "product_role_map",
      "label": "Product-role map"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "PRODUCT_DEFINITION owns urn:stdo:bounded-context:build-tenancy under the selected complete STDO basis. Build tenancy relates one constitutional WHAT to one or more independent project-owned HOW tenants; one tenant is the singleton case and more than one is multi-build-tenancy. SPEC_METHOD §11 owns build tenants, that relation and tenant bindings; TICKETS §4 owns the multi-build-tenant work-item relation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 745,
          "quote": "This member owns the build-tenancy bounded context,\n`urn:stdo:bounded-context:build-tenancy`, under the selected complete STDO\nbasis.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line": 742
        },
        {
          "end_line": 758,
          "quote": "In this context build tenancy is that one-or-more relation; one tenant is\nits singleton case, and multi-build-tenancy begins when the same WHAT has\nmore than one independent realisation",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line": 755
        }
      ],
      "key": "build_tenancy",
      "label": "Build tenancy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "what.intent and what.product each locate one canonical current WHAT entrypoint; what.specification may locate multiple requirement or specification surfaces. how.build_tenants is the canonical Tenant Registry, giving each independent HOW a stable identity and root, design and implementation locators. how.common binds realisation law explicitly adopted by more than one tenant. ticketing.goals locates the work wave; ticketing.tickets the durable root and minimum backlog, active and completed lanes; ticketing.comments the commentary root; optional ticketing.sprints bounded execution-batch manifests.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 763,
          "quote": "`what.intent`, `what.product` and `what.specification` locate the current\nconstitutional WHAT. Intent and Product each have one canonical entrypoint;\nspecification may cite more than one requirement or specification surface",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line": 760
        },
        {
          "end_line": 779,
          "quote": "`ticketing.goals` locates the current work-wave carrier.\n`ticketing.tickets` locates the durable ticket root and the minimum\nbacklog, active and completed lanes. `ticketing.comments` locates the\ncommentary root. `ticketing.sprints` optionally locates bounded\nexecution-batch manifests.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line": 775
        }
      ],
      "key": "what_how_work_locators",
      "label": "WHAT, HOW and work locator roles"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Recursive discovery finds stdo_*.json at the workspace root and arbitrarily deep project roots inside the selected workspace boundary. Explicit composition relates otherwise independent definitions. Each composition entry supplies the target locator, expected stable product.definition_id, authority defining the directed kind with roles, owner, scope, lifecycle and invalidators, and one or more exact governing contracts.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 791,
          "quote": "Tools discover `stdo_*.json` recursively within the selected workspace\nboundary: definitions at the workspace root and definitions at arbitrarily\ndeep nested project roots",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F9.%20Recursive%20discovery%20and%20explicit%20composition",
          "start_line": 788
        },
        {
          "end_line": 806,
          "quote": "Directory nesting creates no implicit inheritance, ownership, composition\nor constitutional override. Parent and child definitions stay independent\nuntil `composition` explicitly relates them.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F9.%20Recursive%20discovery%20and%20explicit%20composition",
          "start_line": 803
        }
      ],
      "key": "definition_discovery_and_composition",
      "label": "Definition discovery and explicit composition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "An exact selected basis is identified."
      ],
      "definition": "Minimum semantic conformance cases cover uniquely resolved uses and material collisions. Without an explicit cross-context relation: Frame evaluation and runtime senses remain distinct; Owner evaluation-family and authority senses remain distinct without authority transfer; immutable Product and mutable Product Definition remain distinct and context-selected; build Tenant supplies no default for hosted, customer, account, runtime or data tenancy; User remains unresolved until downstream authority defines or explicitly relates Product-role, person, principal, account, actor, operator or data-subject meanings.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 863,
          "quote": "Conformance covers both uniquely resolved use and material collision. At\nminimum it establishes these cases under an exact selected basis",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F10.%20Semantic-resolution%20conformance%20cases",
          "start_line": 861
        },
        {
          "end_line": 871,
          "quote": "| `User` | a Product role, human person, identity principal, account, actor, operator or data subject | unresolved until downstream authority defines or explicitly relates the applicable meanings |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F10.%20Semantic-resolution%20conformance%20cases",
          "start_line": 871
        }
      ],
      "key": "semantic_conformance_cases",
      "label": "Semantic-resolution conformance cases"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "STDO-SURFACE-001 makes authorship modular and authority singular: one member owns each law, other members consume it by reference, and members become operative only as a complete released method version. A cut identifies exact members, digests, review basis, human acceptance and immutable identity. Compressions, templates, comments, candidate work, installed copies and mutable source are derived, evidential or authoring surfaces.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 895,
          "quote": "STDO-SURFACE-001. The shared methodology is modular in authorship and\nsingular in authority. Each law has one owning member; other members\nconsume it by reference. The members become operative only as one complete\nreleased version of the method",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F11.%20One%20constitutional%20surface%20and%20version%20boundary",
          "start_line": 885
        }
      ],
      "key": "singular_method_surface",
      "label": "Singular constitutional surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Every Product starts from PRODUCT_DEFINITION_TEMPLATE.json from its selected exact cut, copied to its definition root as stdo_default.json for a singleton default project or stdo_<label>.json for a named definition. Defaults bind the exact installed STDO basis, specification/GOALS.md, INTENT.md, PRODUCT.md, requirements/, build_tenants/ with common/ and default/, and .ai-workspace/ with sprints/, tickets/{backlog,active,completed}/ and comments/. URI bindings determine actual carriers.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 952,
          "quote": "Every product defined under this method begins from\n`templates/PRODUCT_DEFINITION_TEMPLATE.json`, copied to the product's\ndefinition root as `stdo_default.json` for a singleton default project or\nas `stdo_<label>.json` for a named product definition.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F12.%20Bootstrap",
          "start_line": 946
        },
        {
          "end_line": 968,
          "quote": "These are template defaults, not universal path law; the definition's URI\nbindings select the actual project carriers",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F12.%20Bootstrap",
          "start_line": 967
        }
      ],
      "key": "product_bootstrap",
      "label": "Product bootstrap and scaffold"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "PRODUCT_DEFINITION reports conservation of predecessor rows for overlay/layout, required relations, constitutional sufficiency, manager law, complete update, semantic resolution, local binding, collective frames, WHAT/HOW/work carriers, discovery/composition, conformance cases, singular surface and bootstrap. It records ownership migration to this member for product-definition-routing, semantic-resolution and build-tenancy contexts; IDENTITY_METHOD→IDENTITY, RELEASE_METHOD→RELEASE, GLOSSARY_GUIDE→GLOSSARY, REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD→REFERENCE_FRAME and STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE→Executive baseline as carrying rather than dispositions. Design Module Method Ontology is carried as the design profile's accepted boundary ontology. Agent Frame Activation replaces the locally ambiguous phrase execution binding. The mutable method path is generalised to its source workspace. New material includes the §1 family table, cause-specific manager result mapping, and readings of semantic relation kinds in FOUNDATIONS import terms; staleness is a refinement of ACTIVATION §2. These are source conservation claims, not independently established equivalence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1096,
          "quote": "Every row\nis restated in this member's own text; a route is provenance only. Where\nthe predecessor named one of its own members, the tenant member that\nreceives the same law is named instead; this is carrying, not a\ndisposition, and is recorded in the row.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F13.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line": 1092
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1118,
